<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Tarawa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Tarawa was fought from 20 to 23 November 1943 on Betio, an islet of Tarawa atoll in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Marines stormed a strongly fortified Japanese garrison. Nearly all of the roughly 4,500 Japanese defenders and Korean labourers were killed, and about 1,000 Americans died. The heavy losses shaped later American amphibious tactics in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tarawa.jpg" alt="Battle of Tarawa" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: USMC Archives from Quantico, USA · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marines_Advance_on_Japanese_Pill_Boxes,_Tarawa,_November_1943_(10962107133).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Tarawa was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a>, then a Japanese-occupied British colony, and the battle was part of Operation Galvanic, the American invasion of the Gilberts. It was the first American offensive in the Central Pacific. American planners wanted forward air bases to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a>, for which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-islands">Marshall Islands</a> were the nearest base. The Marshalls were cut off from direct communication with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> by a Japanese garrison and air base on Betio, on the western side of Tarawa atoll, so that garrison had to be neutralised first. Betio is about 2 miles long and at most 800 yards wide, and a shallow reef 500 metres wide surrounds it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The invasion force was the largest yet assembled for one Pacific operation, with 17 aircraft carriers, 12 battleships and 36 transports carrying about 35,000 troops of the 2nd Marine Division and the Army's 27th Infantry Division. On 20 November 1943 the island's four 8-inch guns opened fire before dawn, and the battleships <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> silenced three of them. The Japanese fought almost to the last man. Marine losses grew from a miscalculation of the tide and the height of the reef, shortcomings of the landing craft and a bombardment that failed to damage entrenched defences, since high-explosive shells often went off before penetrating them.</p><p>On 21 November the Marines took Green Beach at the western end, while fighting on Red Beaches 2 and 3 was harder. By 22 November they had formed a continuous line across the island, and artillery broke up an expected Japanese counterattack that night. At 03:00 on 23 November a large banzai charge killed 45 Americans and wounded 128, and the Marines killed 325 attackers with support from destroyers. Betio was declared secure at 13:30 on 23 November, about 76 hours after the landing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The 2nd Marine Division lost 894 killed in action, with 84 more dying of wounds, and 2,188 wounded. Only one officer and sixteen enlisted men of the Japanese garrison and 129 of the 1,200 Korean labourers survived. The heavy losses on so small an island prompted public protest, and General Holland Smith likened them to Pickett's Charge. The lessons shaped the Marshalls campaign, where bombardments lasted a month, used armour-piercing shells, and landing craft were armoured. The remains of 36 Marines were recovered in 2015 from a cemetery whose location had been lost.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Tarawa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan occupied the Gilberts in 1941; the <b>Battle of Tarawa</b> in 1943 was among the bloodiest of the Pacific War.</blockquote><a href="/state/gilbert-and-ellice-islands">Gilbert and Ellice Islands</a></li></ul></section>
